# 4. Three-case state machine (bench/references.md §4), as the nearest
#    Phase-1 program.
#
#   λs:Num. if s == 0 then 1 else if s == 1 then 2 else 0
#
# Phase 1 has no sum types and no `match`. The three states are encoded as
# the numeric codes Idle = 0, Running = 1, Stopped = 2, and the match
# becomes a chain of equality tests. What is lost is exhaustiveness: the
# nested `else` is a catch-all, so the compiler can no longer tell you a
# case is missing.

# λs:Num. ⦇⦈
construct-lam
move-parent
set-binder-id 0
set-ann Num
move-child 0

# if s == 0 then 1 else ⦇⦈    -- Idle -> Running
construct-if
construct-binop eq
construct-var 0
move-next-sibling
construct-num 0
move-parent
move-next-sibling
construct-num 1
move-next-sibling

# if s == 1 then 2 else 0     -- Running -> Stopped, Stopped -> Idle
construct-if
construct-binop eq
construct-var 0
move-next-sibling
construct-num 1
move-parent
move-next-sibling
construct-num 2
move-next-sibling
construct-num 0
